  "query_preview": "/Wednesday through Sunday/...\nIssued 314 AM MST SUN NOV 15 2026\nThe brief high pressure ridge over the area will quickly break down \nby Wednesday afternoon, as a closed Pacific upper low steers into \nthe lower northwest and Great Basin. This system will bring a more \nprolonged precipitation event from Wednesday through the end of the \nweek. Guidance continues to suggest the core of the low digging \nsouth of our area, steering abundant wraparound moisture and \ncontinuous precipitation over our area through late Friday. \nContinuous valley rain showers and mountain snow are expected \nWednesday through late Friday. Lower valley areas are tentatively \nlooking at around 0.20 inches of total rainfall. Latest long-range \nguidance has snow levels hanging in the 3500-4500 ft range through \nSaturday. Valleys can see a rain/snow mix, mainly on Friday. Higher \nmountain peaks can still see possible snow accumulations of 5 to 10 \ninches total by early Saturday. Not much cooling is expected with \nthis system, and temperatures are expected to hang near or slightly \nbelow seasonal normals through Friday. Come early Saturday, the \nsystem will steer out of our area as a ridge builds back in. \nSaturday and Sunday will generally see slightly warmer temperatures \nand relatively drier conditions. Another Pacific trough is projected \nto steer into the region, but considerable uncertainty remains over \nits precipitation timing and intensity. For now, late Saturday \nthrough early Sunday will see slight chances of precipitation mainly \nover higher terrain areas.",
